* Continuations after rows that stopped short (NOTES.md #P12, batches 7-10)

Phase 4 (lane D2). Each position below follows a row of ours that had no
continuation, so the opponents' next call (usually a pass) left the system.
The tables give the partnership's next call as the SAYC booklet, or standard
practice where the booklet is silent, has it. Every table partitions the hands
that can reach it: a pass states what it denies.

** Responder after opener's rebid over the Jacoby 2NT (batch 7)

SAYC's Jacoby 2NT is a game-forcing raise with four trumps (13+). Opener's
rebid shows a singleton or void (3 of a new suit), a second five-card suit (4
of it), 18+ (3 of the major), 15--17 (3NT) or a minimum (4 of the major).
Responder signs off in game unless the partnership's values reach slam (about
33 hcp against the range opener has shown); he then bids the small slam
directly (the booklet leaves slam machinery to the partnership).

1M-(P)-2N-(P)-3X-(P)-
6M = {prio:1} 19+ hcp
  #STOP
4M = {prio:0} 13--18 hcp
  #STOP

1M-(P)-2N-(P)-4X-(P)-
6M = {prio:1} 17+ hcp
  #STOP
4M = {prio:0} 13--16 hcp
  #STOP

1M-(P)-2N-(P)-3M-(P)-
6M = {prio:1} 16+ hcp
  #STOP
4M = {prio:0} 13--15 hcp
  #STOP

1M-(P)-2N-(P)-3N-(P)-
6M = {prio:1} 17+ hcp
  #STOP
4M = {prio:0} 13--16 hcp
  #STOP

1M-(P)-2N-(P)-4M-(P)-
6M = {prio:1} 19+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 13--18 hcp
  #STOP

** Responder after the weak two's rebid of its suit (batch 7)

Responder's new suit over a weak two is forcing (10+). The weak two's rebid of
its suit denies support and shows a minimum. Responder bids game in the major
with a doubleton and 15+, 3NT with 15+ otherwise, and passes with less.

2M-(P)-cY-(P)-3M-(P)-
4M = {prio:3} 15+ hcp, 2+M
  #STOP
3N = {prio:2} 15+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 10--14 hcp
  #STOP

2D-(P)-cY-(P)-3D-(P)-
3N = {prio:2} 15+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 10--14 hcp
  #STOP

** Opener after responder's preference or suit rebid (batch 7)

After a non-reverse new suit (12--18), responder's preference to opener's first
suit or the rebid of his own suit is 6--9 (NOTES.md later rounds). Opener passes
a minimum and invites with 16--18: a six-card first suit is raised to the three
level, a five-five hand bids its second suit again, and otherwise 2NT; opposite
responder's rebid, three-card support raises.

1Y-(P)-1Z-(P)-2X-(P)-2Y-(P)-
3Y = {prio:3} 16+ hcp, 6+Y
  #STOP
3X = {prio:2} 16+ hcp, 5+X
  #STOP
2N = {prio:1} 16+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 12--15 hcp
  #STOP

1Y-(P)-1Z-(P)-2X-(P)-2Z-(P)-
3Z = {prio:2} 16+ hcp, 2+Z
  #STOP
2N = {prio:1} 16+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 12--15 hcp
  #STOP

** Opener after a forcing new major over their three-level overcall (batch 7)

Responder's new suit at the three level is forcing (11+, five cards). Opener
raises with three-card support, shows four cards in the other major, bids 3NT
with a stopper in their suit, and otherwise rebids his diamonds.

1D-(3C)-3M-(P)-
4M = {prio:3} 3+M
  #STOP
coM = {prio:2} 4+oM, 0--2M
  #STOP
3N = {prio:1} stopper, 0--2M
  #STOP
4D = {prio:0} 0--2M
  #STOP

** Opener after partner's penalty double of their 1NT overcall (batch 7)

Responder's double of a 1NT overcall is for penalty (9+). Opener sits unless
he has a weak unbalanced opening with a six-card suit, which he rebids.

1X-(1N)-D-(P)-
2X = {prio:1} 12--14 hcp, 6+X, unbal
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 0--5X or 15+ hcp or bal
  #STOP

** The 1NT opener after responder's double of their two-level major (batch 7)

Over their two-level overcall of our 1NT, responder's double is for takeout
(8+, four cards in the other major). Opener bids the other major with four
cards at the cheapest level, passes for penalty with four cards in their suit,
and otherwise bids his longer minor.

1N-(2M)-D-(P)-
coM = {prio:3} 4+oM
  #STOP
P = {prio:2} 4+M, 0--3oM
  #STOP
cm = {prio:1} 4+m, 0--3oM
  #STOP

** After the cue-bid raise of a two-level overcall (batch 7)

The cue bid is a limit raise or better (11+, three-card support). Opener signs
off in three of the major with 12--14 and bids game with 15+; over the sign-off
responder bids game with 13+.

1M-(2X)-3X-(P)-
4M = {prio:1} 15+ hcp
  #STOP
3M = {prio:0} 12--14 hcp

1M-(2X)-3X-(P)-3M-(P)-
4M = {prio:1} 13+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 11--12 hcp
  #STOP

** Advancing a weak jump overcall when they bid on (batch 7)

A weak jump overcall shows a six-card suit. When they bid on at the two or
three level, advancer raises to the three level with three-card support (the
law of total tricks: a nine-card fit competes to the three level), bids game in
a major with 13+ and support (in a minor, four of it), and otherwise passes. `#ANYORDER`: their new suit
may lie above or below their opening suit.

#ANYORDER
(1X)-jM-(2Z/3Z)-
4M = {prio:2} 13+ hcp, 3+M
  #STOP
3M = {prio:1} 3+M
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 0--2M or 0--12 hcp
  #STOP

(1X)-jM-(2X/3X)-
4M = {prio:2} 13+ hcp, 3+M
  #STOP
3M = {prio:1} 3+M
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 0--2M or 0--12 hcp
  #STOP

#ANYORDER
(1X)-jm-(2Z/3Z)-
4m = {prio:2} 13+ hcp, 3+m
  #STOP
3m = {prio:1} 3+m
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 0--2m or 0--12 hcp
  #STOP

(1X)-jm-(2X/3X)-
4m = {prio:2} 13+ hcp, 3+m
  #STOP
3m = {prio:1} 3+m
  #STOP
P = {prio:0} 0--2m or 0--12 hcp
